+When you create `docs/INVENTORY.md`, record each part's delivery kind as a Decisions row that begins `Delivery kind:`, naming each part and its kind's key from the extension-point registry: the kind a type workflow or sub-workflow sets at `intake.delivery-kind`, or else the kind confirmed with the owner at intake (the intake notes). The delivery procedure at project end applies to a part by this recorded kind, and the kind changes only with the part's classification.
+
+### Traits at the design gate
+
+The traits proposed at intake, the default traits of each part's product type and sub-type among them, are settled at this gate (intake's "Classifying each part"). Before presenting the design, weigh each part's traits again against the open list's definitions (`~/.claude/cursor-bridge/workflow/open-list.md`) in the light of the design: a trait the design now shows is added (the product needs accounts: *personal data*), and a proposed or default trait the design shows does not apply is dropped, each with its reason. At the gate each part's traits are a decision of their own, asked in the question form beside the separate decisions the gate below lists: each trait with its definition in one line and, in plain words, the work it adds. Once the owner has settled them, rewrite the part's `Workflows:` line if they changed, and run `python ~/.claude/cursor-bridge/workflow-check.py --status docs/PROJECT_STATUS.md` again.
+
+**A new or unwritten trait is flagged, and the work continues.** A property that adds work and fits no trait's definition is a new trait; a trait whose open-list row reads `no` under Written (the check's `FLAG (trait)`) is defined but not yet written. Traits only add, so the main workflow and the part's product type still cover everything essential: write the extra handling into `docs/DESIGN.md`, a section of its own for each such trait saying what it adds and in which phases, and flag it at the gate as a decision of its own, saying plainly that until the bridge writes the trait's additions the design's section is the only handling it has: the work continues with the handling in the design, or the owner may still choose to pause the project. On a pause the trait goes to the bridge's maintainer as an unwritten product type does at intake: `Awaiting user on: paused at the design gate — <the trait> needs additions the bridge has not written`, the run sheet with your reasons, the release, the calibration, and the owner's word to resume. On resuming, a new trait, now in the open list, is added to each part's `Workflows:` line that has it, the check is run again, and the trait's additions take the place of the design's section. A new trait is not named on the `Workflows:` line until then, since the line names only the open list's entries; its handling lives in the design. Record each flagged trait in `docs/INVENTORY.md` → Decisions as a row that begins `Flagged trait:`, naming the trait, the parts that have it and the design section that handles it, so that the promotion pass at project end puts it forward, from which the maintainer may write it as trait additions; once the trait's additions are written and apply, the row is removed.
